## Task

Formulate between 1 and 3 independent research questions covering:

- Architecture and integration surface the feature touches.
- Failure modes and edge cases the implementation must handle.
- Existing patterns in the workspace or the listed installed skills
  that shape the design.
- Prior work or precedent the design should follow or avoid.

Keep the questions independent so downstream fan-out slots can run in
parallel without a shared dependency.

## Output

```bash
LOOM=~/.kiro/skills/home/loomv2/scripts/loom.sh
$LOOM output init "{{ input.__loom.workdir }}" --task research-plan
$LOOM output add  "{{ input.__loom.workdir }}" --task research-plan \
    --set 'questions[].id=q1' \
    --set 'questions[-1].prompt=<question 1 text>' \
    --set num_questions=1
```

The `questions` array MUST always contain exactly 5 entries
(`id=q1..q5`). Fill the first `num_questions` entries — at most
{{ input.max_questions }} for this run — with real questions.
Prefer USING the full budget: the slots are already paid for in the
graph, and more, narrower questions buy parallelism and depth for
free. Split by code territory — if a candidate question spans
several distinct subsystems (e.g. a wire protocol AND a stats
lookup AND a planner pass), give each subsystem its own question
rather than packing them into one overloaded prompt. Emit fewer
than {{ input.max_questions }} only when the feature genuinely
touches fewer independent territories; set `prompt` to the empty string (`--set-json`) on unused
trailing slots. Unused slots run as no-ops (the slot agent emits empty findings for
an empty prompt); the contract requires explicit empty values, never
omission:

```bash
$LOOM output add  "{{ input.__loom.workdir }}" --task research-plan \
    --set 'questions[].id=q5' --set-json 'questions[-1].prompt=""'
```

Then set `num_questions` to the real-question count.
